    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "71ee719c_4585db15",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 6872,
      "author": {
        "id": 1976445
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T12:09:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What if there are other tasks on the runqueue that are runnable? I think set_tsk_need_resched() will force another pick_next_task_cycle() but you\u0027re just forcing the locks to be released to allow some waiters to obtain it. Can we enter this path more than once due to unexpected delay in the remote CPU to acquire the lock fast enough?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6872,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 6872,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "ff90f077ac26a64036249739b2a17d7fc94d1124",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "236ccd2a_36298574",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 6872,
      "author": {
        "id": 1945845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T21:53:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah. So it will context switch to schedule idle for a moment and then because need_resched is preserved, it will trip into schedule again.  It seems unlikely we would be able to do all that while the remote CPU is spinning on the lock, but with crazy numa topologies it seems possible. I\u0027ve not noticed it myself but its something I can try to watch for.",
      "parentUuid": "71ee719c_4585db15",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6872,
